// The shape the lint missed for as long as it existed: `for x in snapshot(b)` inside a loop that
// keeps appending to `b`. It only ever looked at a *declaration's* initialiser, and converting a
// handle in order to look through it is exactly what a `for` wants -- so this is arguably the more
// common way to write the quadratic. Three live instances were sitting unflagged in the reference
// backend, two of them a linear membership scan over a full copy per pass.
//
// The advice is different for this shape, and better: all four mutable collections iterate directly
// as of F111, so the fix is to drop the conversion rather than to reach for a reader.
//
// **`tally` is flagged too, and that is the 2026-11-13 change.** The rule above only ever ran on a
// loop's body, so a `for x in snapshot(b)` anywhere else -- including at the top of a function --
// was never looked at, and the shape went on being written: twelve instances were sitting in
// `lib/` unflagged, six of them real. A `for` reads all four mutable collections directly, so the
// conversion copies the whole builder to read it whatever encloses it.
//
// `grow` below is the one that must stay unflagged, and it is the opposite case: a loop whose own
// body adds to what it is iterating. There the copy is the point, and advising it away would be
// advising a different program.
fn first_duplicate(names: [string]) -> string {
    val seen = mutable_array_new<string>()
    for name in names {
        for already in snapshot(seen) {
            if already == name {
                return name
            }
        }
        push(seen, name)
    }
    return ""
}

fn tally(words: [string]) -> int {
    val counts = mutable_map_new<string, int>()
    for w in words {
        set(counts, w, 1)
    }
    var n = 0
    for k, v in snapshot(counts) {
        n = n + v
    }
    return n
}

fn main() {
    print(first_duplicate(["a", "b", "a"]))
    print(tally(["a", "b"]))
}

// The exemption: the body appends to the collection being iterated, so the copy is what stops the
// loop from seeing what it adds.
fn grow(seeds: [int]) -> int {
    val items = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for s in seeds {
        push(items, s)
    }
    for x in snapshot(items) {
        push(items, x + 1)
    }
    return len(items)
}
